{"subject":"Pathology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Pathology::76.WBC_Basics_and_Instruments","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532536","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":1,"stemHtml":"A 50-year-old man presents with complaints of fatigue, easy bruising, and recurrent nosebleeds over the past month. Laboratory investigations reveal leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. Peripheral blood smear examination demonstrates the presence of cells with Auer rods. In which stage of myelopoiesis are Auer rods typically found?<br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q001-p1611-stem-1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myeloblast"},{"label":"B","text":"Myelocyte"},{"label":"C","text":"Promyelocyte"},{"label":"D","text":"Metamyelocyte"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Myeloblast","solutionHtml":"2777193037caba514\"&amp;gt;Myeloid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4521dbf4b91692777191a73002bf98\">Leukemia</span> (AML<strong>)</strong>, characterised by <strong>Myeloblasts with Auer rods,</strong> which are needle-like granules in the cytoplasm.\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Sarita Pradhan\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.5\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Myeloblasts-and-plasma-cells-containing-Auer-rod-like-inclusions-Figure-2-Plasma-cell_fig1_295830389\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"304\" src=\"images/GqeSBLcUWkkPX15kBj9C1737016463.png\"\" width=\"400\"/></p>\n<p>Myelopoiesis is the process of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"09be7d1f11169277720210bf5b0280\">WBC</span> formation, which starts with the <strong>Pluripotent Stem Cells,</strong><strong> which are </strong><strong>CD34+.</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"Hematopoiesis_\" data-author=\"Original: A. Rad Vector: RexxS, birdy and Mikael Häggström, M.D.\" data-hash=\"816\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granulopoiesis#/media/File:Hematopoiesis_(human)_diagram_en.svg\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"479\" src=\"images/yuBR89jH9WSQVIZ9ih081737653029.png\"\" width=\"600\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/GqeSBLcUWkkPX15kBj9C1737016463.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/yuBR89jH9WSQVIZ9ih081737653029.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 585<br/>Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology, 14th edition, Page 449-450.<br/>Auer rods<br/>Image source:https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Band_neutrophil_%2816694694661%29.jpg</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532538","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with complaints of persistent fever, chills, and generalized weakness for the past week. Lab reports revealed leukocytosis with a left shift, and peripheral blood smear examination shows cells with kidney-shaped nuclei and parasites in ring forms inside RBCs. What is the cell called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monocyte"},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophil"},{"label":"C","text":"Basophil"},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophil"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Monocyte","solutionHtml":"<p>The patient most likely has <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8b4c650ddd1692777191286afbcd8a\">malaria</span> which presents initially with fever, chills and weakness. On <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6777533153169277719804fcbbf2d1\">smear</span> examination, there is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cc8beb11041692777192b19dfb290a\">monocytosis</span> (cells with kidney-shaped nuclei) and parasites in ring forms within RBCs.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Different types of WBC</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Zahra Mousavi Kouzehkanan,Sepehr Saghari,Sajad Tavakoli,Peyman Rostami,Mohammadjavad Abaszadeh,Farzaneh Mirzadeh,Esmaeil Shahabi Satlsar\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Five-types-of-white-blood-cells-in-the-normal-peripheral-blood_fig1_358024806\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"300\" src=\"images/06kfZPqUb4YWnEozGjCr1737016960.png\"\" width=\"700\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>a) Granulocytes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neutrophils: </strong>3-5 lobes and are involved in the<strong> </strong><strong>acute <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9d21dd5d3116927771907405b181bc\">inflammatory</span> response</strong> to bacterial infection and the removal of the bacteria by phagocytosis.<strong> </strong><strong>(Option B)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Eosinophils</strong><strong>: </strong>They have <strong>orange-red granules</strong> and a bilobed <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ff628904401692777193c8a9f9159e\">nucleus</span> and are involved in chronic inflammation, <strong>allergic reactions,</strong> and host defence against <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c0433f52ad1692777194c2ffb6fc63\">parasitic</span> infections. <strong>(Option D)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Basophils: </strong>They<strong> </strong>have <strong>blue-black granules</strong> and their primary function is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e747a47db616927771908bff4b8931\">inflammation</span> and allergic reaction. They act similar to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1f95a52091169277719133cc234fe0\">Mast</span> cells.<strong> (Option C)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>b) Agranulocytes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymphocytes: </strong>They are part of the adaptive immune system. They are circulating<strong> immunocompetent cells</strong> that develop the ability to recognise and react to antigens.</li>\n<li><strong>Monocytes:</strong> They have a kidney-shaped nucleus. In tissues, they <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c9001edbf216927771860f3475b44c\">differentiate</span> into <strong>phagocytes</strong>, such as macrophages, osteoclasts and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cc2e86ef7e1692777191151f919395\">Kupffer cells</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d1a139c3bf16927771954479445976\">phagocytose</span> bacteria, cells, and debris and function as antigen-presenting cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/06kfZPqUb4YWnEozGjCr1737016960.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 586<br/>Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 14th Edition, Page 450.<br/>WBC Histology<br/>Types of WBCs</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532540","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old child is brought up due to concerns about persistent fever, fatigue, and frequent infections over the past few weeks. There is generalised lymphadenopathy. Laboratory investigations reveal leukocytosis with lymphoblasts on peripheral smear examination. Which of the following stains are used to visualise lymphoblasts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Periodic Acid Schiff (PAS) stain"},{"label":"B","text":"Myeloperoxidase stain"},{"label":"C","text":"Sudan Black stain"},{"label":"D","text":"Wright stain"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Periodic Acid Schiff (PAS) stain","solutionHtml":"data-dictid=\"1622f69a94169277719372b76236ca\"&amp;gt;neoplasm composed of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c219021c491692777189d289f1fc03\">immature</span> B or T cells, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"047f8930dd1692777197fed61e213c\">referred</span> to as<strong> </strong><strong>Lymphoblasts which is visualised by </strong>Periodic Acid Schiff (PAS) stain\n<li>Symptoms related to depression of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c8a8c935a41692777191610f6aacb4\">marrow</span> function and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c82360aadd16927771936d67fa30a4\">neoplastic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"669bdcb3111692777190e018ae930e\">infiltration</span> include fatigue (anaemia), fever(infections due to neutropenia), <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"41e858bf2816927771836d46c619b9\">bleeding</span> (thrombocytopenia), generalised lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and hepatomegaly, most of which are seen in this patient.</li>\n\n<p><br/>\n </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:644px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Lymphoblast</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Myeloblast</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Monoblasts</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Cell Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>No Auer rods</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Auer rods present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Lobulated <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e6b47028191692777193eb698e43b6\">nuclei</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c9001edbf216927771860f3475b44c\">differentiate</span> it from myeloblasts.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Stains</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Periodic Acid Schiff (PAS) stain</li>\n<li>Acid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"65f1c4f3d4169277719505249503e0\">phosphatase</span> for T-lymphoblasts</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Myeloperoxidase <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b29a1a16ab1692777199ede1c6e28b\">stain</span> <strong> </strong><strong>(Option B)</strong></li>\n<li>Sudan Black <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b29a1a16ab1692777199ede1c6e28b\">stain</span> <strong>(Option C)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>HPE</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\" VashiDonsk\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leukemia\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"227\" src=\"images/BYrJlpNBIBAuZL2CX1th1737018524.png\"\" width=\"200\"/></p>\n<p>Nucleus with clumped, dark purple chromatin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Sarita Pradhan\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.5\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Myeloblasts-and-plasma-cells-containing-Auer-rod-like-inclusions-Figure-2-Plasma-cell_fig1_295830389\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"152\" src=\"images/au6AtVrJuY1WNH1ZiC0r1737018453.png\"\" width=\"200\"/></p>\n<p>Nucleus with opened up, homogeneous, lighter chromatin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monoblast\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"186\" src=\"images/3qTnmBwEJ4hAC0Sk9Zac1737018471.png\"\" width=\"200\"/></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Wright's stain</strong> <strong>(Option D):</strong> Wright’s <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b29a1a16ab1692777199ede1c6e28b\">stain</span> is a <strong>mixture of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2379e628e2169277718790981be825\">eosin</span> (red) and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f1f83adcbd1692777192944952fb13\">methylene</span> blue dyes </strong>and is used in blood smears and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b9831f3aa169277718395d7361df6\">bone marrow</span> aspirates to detect RBCs, WBCs and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e7032bae7f1692777195d693722e54\">platelet</span> abnormalities.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/BYrJlpNBIBAuZL2CX1th1737018524.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/au6AtVrJuY1WNH1ZiC0r1737018453.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/3qTnmBwEJ4hAC0Sk9Zac1737018471.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 594, 596<br/>Monoblast<br/>WBC<br/>Lymphoblastic lymphoma</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532542","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":4,"stem":"A 55-year-old male presents with persistent fatigue, significant weight loss, and night sweats over the past few months. Examination revealed massive splenomegaly. CBC shows elevated white blood cell count. A bone marrow biopsy confirms the diagnosis of CML. Which of the following types of leukocytosis is most commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophilia"},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophilia"},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocytosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Basophilia"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Basophilia","solutionHtml":"(basophilia) is a condition characterised by an increased number of basophils in the blood.\n<li>This is a rare finding, most commonly associated with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8dd789d7d1169277719303d4744837\">myeloproliferative</span> neoplasms, such as<strong> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"dc80b4e7901692777184c5b45d1bf5\">Chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"da88d7f2211692777193037caba514\">Myeloid</span> Leukemia</span> (CML). </strong></li>\n\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:612px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Type of Leukocytosis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Causes</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Neutrophilic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"515cde208f1692777191112bb2ff9e\">leukocytosis</span> <strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute bacterial infections</strong> (e.g., <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f6332ef2d21692777196294f154e57\">pyogenic</span> organisms);</li>\n<li>Sterile <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e747a47db616927771908bff4b8931\">inflammation</span> (e.g., tissue necrosis)</li>\n<li>Burns, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"340e5cd49c16927771917ee55b9e76\">Leukemoid</span> reactions</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Eosinophilic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"515cde208f1692777191112bb2ff9e\">leukocytosis</span> (eosinophilia) <strong>(Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Allergic disorders</strong> (e.g., asthma);</li>\n<li>Parasitic infestations</li>\n<li>Hodgkin Lymphoma</li>\n<li>Hay fever</li>\n<li>Tropical <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"10e2dee19e1692777196673db90c23\">Pulmonary</span> eosinophilia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Basophilic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"515cde208f1692777191112bb2ff9e\">leukocytosis</span> (basophilia)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Myeloproliferative <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1622f69a94169277719372b76236ca\">neoplasm</span> (e.g., <strong>chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"da88d7f2211692777193037caba514\">myeloid</span> leukaemia</strong>)</li>\n<li>Polycythaemia vera</li>\n<li>Myelofibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Monocytosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic infections (e.g.,<strong> </strong><strong>tuberculosis</strong>);</li>\n<li>Autoimmune disorders (e.g., <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8f2e7fd96116927771995b451b5c90\">systemic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ccd536a9071692777191f53538ca30\">lupus</span> erythematosus)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Lymphocytosis <strong>(Option C)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Viral infections</strong><strong> </strong>(e.g., Epstein-Barr virus);</li>\n<li>Chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fced475d231692777189effb72c5c7\">immunologic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7f3792072b1692777199fd8e2c4f59\">stimulation</span> (e.g., tuberculosis)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 588</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532544","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":5,"stemHtml":"Match the following cells with their respective images\n\t\t\ta) Hypersegmented neutrophil\n\t\t\t1)\n\t\t\tb) Hyposegmented neutrophil\n\t\t\t2)\n\t\t\tc) Dohle bodies\n\t\t\t3)\n\t\t\td) Alder Reilly anomaly\n\t\t\t4)<br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-2.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-3.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-4.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2"},{"label":"B","text":"a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3"},{"label":"C","text":"a-4, b-3, c-1, d-2"},{"label":"D","text":"a-2, b-3, c-4, d-1"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2","solutionHtml":"border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"&amp;gt;\n\t\t\t<p><strong>Pathological features</strong></p>\n\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>\n</td>\n\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Hypersegmented neutrophil</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"Hypersegmented neutrophil\" data-author=\"Ed Uthman\" data-hash=\"817\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypersegmented_neutrophil\" data-tags=\"January2025\" src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-3.jpg\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hypersegmented neutrophils are characterized by nuclear lobes equal to or more than 5.</li>\n<li>They are typically seen in conditions such as <strong>megaloblastic anaemia,</strong><strong> </strong>which is often due to deficiencies in vitamin B12 or folate.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Hyposegmented neutrophil</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"988\" src=\"images/op42Ps7vwszKEAfrDGU91737657584.png\"\" width=\"702\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hyposegmented neutrophils show bilobed or round <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e6b47028191692777193eb698e43b6\">nuclei</span> instead of the typical segmented appearance.</li>\n<li>This <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"50a440f85816927771817d2fe50984\">anomaly</span> can occur as an inherited trait <strong>(Pelger-Huet anomaly)</strong><strong> </strong>or as a secondary finding in various syndromes such as <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"29948ab80216927771932de0443c18\">Myelodysplastic</span> syndrome, where it is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"047f8930dd1692777197fed61e213c\">referred</span> to as Pseudo-Pelger-Huet anomaly.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Dohle bodies</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"Dohle bodies\" data-author=\"Ibrahim Abukhiran Judy Jasser Sergei I Syrbu\" data-hash=\"818\" data-license=\"CC BY NC 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Neutrophils-showing-toxic-granulation-Doehle-bodies-and-cytoplasmic-vacuolations_fig2_338618310\" data-tags=\"January2025\" src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-1.jpg\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dohle bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions seen in neutrophils.</li>\n<li>Typically observed in patients with severe sepsis.</li>\n<li>Dohle bodies represent remnants of rough <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c01a5a8c221692777187daa368ab62\">endoplasmic reticulum</span> and are indicative of ongoing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12a6f23332169277718402ca6703e2\">cellular</span> stress or activation.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Alder Reilly anomaly</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"Alder Reilly anomaly\" data-author=\"Nour Hisham Almozain Nasir Bakshi\" data-hash=\"819\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/a-Prominent-dark-staining-and-coarse-cytoplasmic-granules-in-polymorphonuclear_fig1_316069107\" data-tags=\"January2025\" src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-2.jpg\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alder-Reilly <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"50a440f85816927771817d2fe50984\">anomaly</span> is characterised by large cytoplasmic granules seen in neutrophils.</li>\n<li>This <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"50a440f85816927771817d2fe50984\">anomaly</span> is associated with <strong>mucopolysaccharidoses,</strong> a group of inherited <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d92cbff33316927771927b4de10a5b\">metabolic</span> disorders characterised by the accumulation of glycosaminoglycans in various tissues due to deficient lysosomal enzymes.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n<br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-3.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/op42Ps7vwszKEAfrDGU91737657584.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l071-q005-p1613-stem-2.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 688<br/>Neutrophil nuclear morphology<br/>Alder-Reilly anomaly</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532546","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":6,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy presented with complaints of a sore throat, fever, and extreme fatigue for the past two weeks. On examination, he has enlarged cervical lymph nodes and an enlarged spleen. A complete blood count reveals lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes and is diagnosed as infectious mononucleosis. Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused by Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"},{"label":"B","text":"The Monospot test is a common diagnostic tool"},{"label":"C","text":"Downey cells are characteristic histopathological features."},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as Haemophagocytic Lymphohistiocytosis."}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Also known as Haemophagocytic Lymphohistiocytosis.","solutionHtml":"pan class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8505361aed1692777191a3bd758c12\"&amp;gt;lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"092583d1b41692777197a506409267\">reactive</span> condition marked by cytopenias and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8f2e7fd96116927771995b451b5c90\">systemic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e747a47db616927771908bff4b8931\">inflammation</span> due to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2748357f451692777191377abbe2a6\">macrophage</span> activation.\n<li>It is caused by the dysregulated <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ba6b77f29316927771807350f86ce2\">activation</span> of<strong> </strong><strong>NK cells, CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Epstein-Barr virus</strong><strong> </strong>infection is a common trigger.</li>\n<li>Clinical features include <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4cdc8161f71692777180df58cafa93\">acute</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e57922b3c91692777202151895d37e\">febrile</span> illness, splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, anaemia, thrombocytopenia, elevated <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"91d0e4c6fa16927772025f96b44b27\">ferritin</span> and IL-2 <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9e4959f6f316927771978bb152d440\">receptor</span> levels, and liver dysfunction.</li>\n<li><strong>Infectious mononucleosis, </strong>primarily caused by<strong> </strong><strong>Epstein-Barr Virus</strong><strong> </strong><strong>(Option A),</strong> presents with fever, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0d8cd9c1b516927771981287d4850f\">sore</span> throat, lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly, predominantly affecting late adolescents and young adults.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis relies on <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3a521c75816927771913d4e993e2d\">lymphocytosis</span> with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"06123ff61a1692777182e19b790ebc\">atypical</span> lymphocytes, known as <strong>Downey cells (Ballerina skirt appearance) </strong><strong> </strong><strong>(Option C),</strong><strong> </strong>which are<strong> </strong><strong>activated <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"07fe9383c5169277718339acdb5736\">CD8 T lymphocytes</span> </strong>responding to EBV-infected cells.</li>\n<li>Positive <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8ca4dcc0c1169277718848b8f5ae3d\">heterophile</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9d7a12cab116927771811a0079086d\">antibody</span> reaction <strong>(Monospot test)</strong><strong> </strong><strong>(Option B) </strong>and rising specific antibodies for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e4f6b2922d16927771865030175a86\">EBV</span> antigens aid in diagnosis.</li>\n\n<p> </p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Bobjgalindo\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactive_lymphocyte\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"550\" src=\"images/Dyi5anrDKwEPzbuVfi4S1737089672.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p><br/><img src=\"images/Dyi5anrDKwEPzbuVfi4S1737089672.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 358</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532548","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":7,"stem":"A 58-year-old patient presents with fatigue, night sweats, and significant weight loss. Physical examination reveals generalized lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. Blood tests show a markedly elevated white blood cell count. Based on the clinical scenario, which diagnosis is most likely if the bone marrow biopsy reveals over 20% blasts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)"},{"label":"B","text":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)"},{"label":"C","text":"Hodgkin’s Lymphoma (HL)"},{"label":"D","text":"Non-Hodgkin’s Lymphoma (NHL)"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)","solutionHtml":"1dbf4b91692777191a73002bf98\"&amp;gt;Leukemia (AML)\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Based on the clinical scenario and the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b9831f3aa169277718395d7361df6\">bone marrow</span> findings, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>Acute <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"da88d7f2211692777193037caba514\">Myeloid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4521dbf4b91692777191a73002bf98\">Leukemia</span> (AML). </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Blasts in the bone marrow</strong> are a pivotal factor in diagnosing different types of leukaemia.</li>\n<li>When the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b9831f3aa169277718395d7361df6\">bone marrow</span> contains over 20% blasts, it indicates <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4cdc8161f71692777180df58cafa93\">acute</span> leukaemia rather than a chronic condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"da88d7f2211692777193037caba514\">Myeloid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4521dbf4b91692777191a73002bf98\">Leukemia</span> (AML)</strong> is characterised by the rapid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8eedb6cec61692777196a6a3b93d3f\">proliferation</span> of <strong>abnormal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"da88d7f2211692777193037caba514\">myeloid</span> blasts </strong>(Myeloblasts are identified by the presence of Auer rods), <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e4f649e27616927771856e338fa9f8\">crowding</span> out normal cells and leading to the symptoms observed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"The Armed Forces Institute of Pathology (AFIP)\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"Public domain\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auer_rod\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"378\" src=\"images/yw5a75HidpNZne0GB5hQ1737088249.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Chronic Lymphocytic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4521dbf4b91692777191a73002bf98\">Leukemia</span> (CLL) (Option A):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CLL </strong>typically presents with<strong> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0472620c171692777192c655af9c57\">mature</span> lymphocytes</strong>, not blasts, in the bone marrow, distinguishing it from AML.</li>\n<li>Histopathology reveals <strong>smudge cells.</strong> The elevated blast count seen in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b9831f3aa169277718395d7361df6\">bone marrow</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"754ab07a2e1692777183ed178c883d\">biopsy</span> is not characteristic of CLL.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Mikael Häggström, M.D.\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smudge_cell_in_a_peripheral_blood_smear.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"435\" src=\"images/hpUSQeMBy5YhcAlhfNHk1737088313.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Hodgkin’s <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5a31d184f21692777191a2e457d646\">Lymphoma</span> (Option C): </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>HL is diagnosed via <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"754ab07a2e1692777183ed178c883d\">biopsy</span> revealing <strong>Reed-Sternberg cells</strong><strong>,</strong> not through a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b9831f3aa169277718395d7361df6\">bone marrow</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"754ab07a2e1692777183ed178c883d\">biopsy</span> showing high blast counts.</li>\n<li>The hallmark of HL is the presence of specific large <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"458e050fdc16927771809854c9fd53\">abnormal</span> lymphocytes, not blasts.HL primarily involves <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> nodes, with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8f2e7fd96116927771995b451b5c90\">systemic</span> symptoms such as <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a696a8e86016927771939c84736fbb\">night sweats</span> and weight loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Unknown\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"Public domain\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reed%E2%80%93Sternberg_cell\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"321\" src=\"images/fjenXlI5DjsNJJNEalRu1737089547.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Non-Hodgkin’s <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5a31d184f21692777191a2e457d646\">Lymphoma</span> (NHL) (Option D):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The diagnosis of NHL involves<strong> </strong><strong>malignant <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"32599f889a169277719169e3483e62\">lymphoid</span> cells</strong> in affected tissues, not an increased blast percentage in the bone marrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Karim Ibn majdoub hassani,Hicham Elbouhaddouti,Abdelmalek Ousadden,Amal Ankouz,Meryem Boubbou,S. Tizniti,Khalid Mazaz,Khalid Ait Taleb\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Histological-examination-demonstrating-non-Hodgkins-lymphoma-of-the-diffuse-large-B-cell_fig7_49647373\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"400\" src=\"images/InlJrinuNrdRjbU3rcJN1737089586.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p><br/><img src=\"images/yw5a75HidpNZne0GB5hQ1737088249.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/hpUSQeMBy5YhcAlhfNHk1737088313.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/fjenXlI5DjsNJJNEalRu1737089547.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/InlJrinuNrdRjbU3rcJN1737089586.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran, Pathological Basis of Disease, 10th Edition, Page 617</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532550","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":8,"stem":"A 45-year-old female is undergoing routine blood work. The laboratory technician uses different Vacutainers for various tests. Which Vacutainer colour is used for coagulation studies, such as PT and aPTT?","options":[],"answer":"","answerText":"","solutionHtml":"ta-dictid=\"42f1e4deb8169277718412c0681a54\"&amp;gt;coagulation studies, such as<strong> </strong><strong>Prothrombin Time (PT) and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0f61fd74c41692777180190320f12e\">Activated Partial <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b51982d06169277720063c9420fbb\">Thromboplastin</span> Time</span> (aPTT)</strong><strong>.</strong> The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b5fc7729f316927771817f809648f5\">anticoagulant</span> preserves the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"42f1e4deb8169277718412c0681a54\">coagulation</span> factors for accurate testing.<br/>\n \n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Colour</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Uses</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Purple/Lavender<strong> </strong><strong>(Option D)</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"129\" src=\"images/PaKneiv8BgE2iRY6D8ls1739421001.png\"\" width=\"400\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>EDTA (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>CBC, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"255674f3271692777195c172d86daf\">Peripheral</span> Smear, ESR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Light Blue (Option B) </strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"322\" src=\"images/kHqfIHZqA9h3bhHnQqXt1739421049.png\"\" width=\"200\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Citrate (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>ESR, PT, aPTT</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Red <strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"400\" src=\"images/nfsJ1KURbpKnsKNc7xAt1739421144.png\"\" width=\"200\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>No anti-coagulant is added</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Yellow</p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"400\" src=\"images/JLnbRFll3leogqClnhrV1739420920.png\"\" width=\"150\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Clot activator/Gel separator</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Grey</p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"400\" src=\"images/a2FVr0kIPVj5ckPZ70FT1739420859.png\"\" width=\"150\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Sodium Fluoride</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Blood Glucose</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Green <strong>(Option C)</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"Green vacutainer tube\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"820\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" src=\"images/sd3YwppES9jV7XpfOnLR1737658052.png\"\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Heparin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>ABG, Osmotic fragility, flow cytometry, immunophenotyping</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><img src=\"images/PaKneiv8BgE2iRY6D8ls1739421001.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/kHqfIHZqA9h3bhHnQqXt1739421049.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/nfsJ1KURbpKnsKNc7xAt1739421144.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/JLnbRFll3leogqClnhrV1739420920.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/a2FVr0kIPVj5ckPZ70FT1739420859.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/sd3YwppES9jV7XpfOnLR1737658052.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Atlas of Clinical Hematology, Chapter 1, Page 4<br/>Laboratory tube collection</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532552","topic":"PrepLadder · 76.WBC Basics and Instruments","number":9,"stem":"Which of the following diluting fluids correctly matches its specific use in haematology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turk's Solution - Platelets"},{"label":"B","text":"Hayem's Solution - WBCs"},{"label":"C","text":"Gower's Solution - RBCs"},{"label":"D","text":"1% Ammonium Oxalate - WBCs"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Gower's Solution - RBCs","solutionHtml":"i&amp;gt;<strong>Gower's solution is specifically designed for the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e58c34390016927771859e87c91ed7\">counting</span> of RBCs.</strong>\n<li>It provides a suitable environment for diluting blood samples while preserving the integrity and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2dcf62bc831692777192e737f2c4b0\">morphology</span> of red blood cells.</li>\n<li>This solution ensures accurate <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e58c34390016927771859e87c91ed7\">counting</span> of RBCs under a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"195d6a11ca16927771927a92617498\">microscope</span> without altering their appearance or function.</li>\n\n<p><strong>Turk's Solution - Platelets (Option A): </strong>Turk's solution causes <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c2b082746716927771888f0933941d\">hemolysis</span> of red blood cells and stains white blood cells for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"79067cb33316927771926965c3c0b5\">microscopic</span> examination, not for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e7032bae7f1692777195d693722e54\">platelet</span> counting.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Hayem's Solution - WBCs (Option B): </strong>Hayem’s solution is used for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8790b4667016927771979066e8958c\">RBC</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e58c34390016927771859e87c91ed7\">counting</span> rather than <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"09be7d1f11169277720210bf5b0280\">WBC</span> due to its <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"387fabe9051692777190ac45af2fe8\">isotonicity</span> preventing hemolysis, fixation, maintaining cell shape, and prevention of agglutination, with a longer shelf life.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>1% <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3719e878a4169277718109b2d3f67e\">Ammonium</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"28ddbfbaa51692777194aa6875fac4\">Oxalate</span> - WBCs (Option D): </strong>Ammonium <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"28ddbfbaa51692777194aa6875fac4\">oxalate</span> is used to prevent <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"58c17961c71692777184ab55a9c4df\">clotting</span> in blood collection tubes and does not directly aid in white <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"54dd4915601692777183af673aaf7b\">blood cell count</span> in haematology.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Atlas of Clinical Hematology, Chapter 3</div>","tags":""}]}